DB Browsers Execute-SQL runs the query on a QThread subclass (RunSql)
and blocks on std::condition_variable between statements, waiting for
the main thread to call startNextStatement() from a signal handler.
Qt-wasm without pthreads never runs QThread::run(), so hitting Execute
or Ctrl+Enter does nothing.

On wasm, override QThread::start() to run the statement loop
synchronously on the calling thread and emit finished() at the end.
Skip the inter-statement cv.wait() since there is no other thread
that could call startNextStatement(); signals still fire via
QueuedConnection so slots update the UI on the next event-loop tick.

--- a/src/RunSql.h
+++ b/src/RunSql.h
@@ -49,6 +49,10 @@ public:
 
     void startNextStatement();
 
+#ifdef __EMSCRIPTEN__
+    void start();
+#endif
+
     void stop();
 
 signals:
--- a/src/RunSql.cpp
+++ b/src/RunSql.cpp
@@ -56,6 +56,21 @@ void RunSql::run()
         emit structureUpdated();
 }
 
+#ifdef __EMSCRIPTEN__
+void RunSql::start()
+{
+    // Wasm without pthreads: run the statement loop synchronously on the
+    // calling thread. Signals still fire via QueuedConnection so slots
+    // update the UI on the next event-loop tick, matching the threaded
+    // contract.
+    run();
+    // QThread::finished is a QPrivateSignal so subclasses can't emit it
+    // directly; fire it through the meta-object system (queued so slots
+    // run once the caller returns to the event loop).
+    QMetaObject::invokeMethod(this, "finished", Qt::QueuedConnection);
+}
+#endif
+
 void RunSql::startNextStatement()
